Step 7: Open Jupyter and select the kernel 
1. Launch Jupyter as you normally do. 
2.Create a new notebook and choose Python (tf_env) from the New menu, or use Kernel → Change kernel in an existing notebook.
3. Check that the kernel name at the top right reads Python (tf_env). 

Step 8: Verify the setup Run this in a notebook cell: 
import numpy as np, sklearn 
import tensorflow as tf from tensorflow 
import keras  
print("TensorFlow:", tf.__version__) 
print("NumPy:", np.__version__) 
print("scikit-learn:", sklearn.__version__)  
model = keras.Sequential([     
keras.layers.Input(shape=(4,)),     
keras.layers.Dense(8, activation="relu"),     
keras.layers.Dense(1, activation="sigmoid"), ]) 
model.compile(optimizer="adam", loss="binary_crossentropy") 
model.fit(np.random.rand(20, 4), np.random.randint(0, 2, 20), epochs=2) 
print("Setup works!") 
#If it trains for two epochs and prints Setup works!, you are ready. Warnings about CPU instructions or oneDNN are harmless. 
