Rules to Avoid NumPy and scikit-learn Problems 
• Do not install or upgrade NumPy yourself. TensorFlow installs the right version. 
• Install TensorFlow first, then everything else. • Do not mix conda install and pip install for these libraries in the same environment. 
• Never run pip install --upgrade numpy . 

Troubleshooting 
Problem Fix No matching distribution found for tensorflow-> Run python --version. It should show 3.11. Recreate the environment with Step 2. 
ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'tensorflow' -> The notebook is on the wrong kernel. Switch to Python (tf_env).
Kernel tf_env not listed in Jupyter -> Run jupyter kernelspec list. If it is missing, repeat Step 6 with (tf_env) active, then restart Jupyter. 
pip fails on the whole install line -> Check the package names for typos (for example matplotlib, not matplotlip). 
NumPy-related error such as "numpy.dtype size changed" -> Create a fresh environment and follow the steps in order without extra installs. 


Notes 
• No conda available? Use python -m venv tf_env, then activate it (tf_env\Scripts\activate on Window). Continue from Step 4.
• GPU: Native Windows does not support TensorFlow GPU. CPU is enough so ignore the warning. 
• To Save your setup: pip freeze > requirements.txt records the installed versions so you can rebuild the environment later. 
• For Removing the environment: conda remove -n tf_env --all. 
